# Debian preseed for a calango-nix machine, generated by home/bootstrap.nix.
#
# This file drives the three things the flake already declares -- the Stage A
# package list, the group memberships, and the account name -- and REFUSES to
# drive the disk. Partitioning, locale, mirror, timezone and the root password
# are the installer's own prompts, and they stay that way on purpose: they are
# decisions about a physical disk, and suffer is LUKS-encrypted, so a generated
# recipe would either not match this project's convention or would have to apply
# encryption unattended to hardware.
#
# Serve it and boot the installer against it:
#
#   B=$(sg nix-users -c 'nix build --no-link --print-out-paths .#calangoBootstrap')
#   (cd "$B" && python3 -m http.server 8000)
#
#   auto=true url=http://<this-machine>:8000/preseed.cfg
#
# No priority=critical: that would suppress every question below critical,
# answering each from its installer default -- including disk, locale, mirror
# and timezone, which RUNBOOK.md's Stage 0 tells the reader to answer by hand.
# auto=true alone still fetches and applies this file. See RUNBOOK.md's Stage 0
# for the account-question measurement that makes dropping it safe.
#
# It is unauthenticated http on a LAN for the minutes an install takes, and it
# contains no secret. Do not leave it running afterwards.

# The account name is generated from the flake's home.username, because
# everything later resolves through it: the generation is built for
# <user>@<host>, and a mismatch here means nothing in Stage B applies.
d-i passwd/username string @username@

# Stage A's package list, generated from calango.bootstrap.packages.base.
#
# An unavailable name here FAILS the install rather than warning. Measured on a
# Debian 13.6 netinst with one deliberately bogus name added to the real list:
#
#   [!!] Select and install software
#        Installation step failed
#        The failing step is: Select and install software
#
# The installer halts at that dialog. That is why this file needs no separate
# gate proving the packages landed -- a typo cannot produce a running machine
# that is missing one.
d-i pkgsel/include string @basePackages@

# No desktop task. The desktop is Nix's, and tasksel's would install a second one.
tasksel tasksel/first multiselect standard

# "none" is debian-installer's non-default: left unset it defaults to
# safe-upgrade, pulling in whatever point-release fixes exist at install time.
# This file chooses "none" deliberately rather than leaving the question to the
# installer's own default, for the same reason Stage 0 answers disk, locale,
# mirror and timezone by hand instead of preseeding them -- an unattended
# upgrade during install is a behaviour this flake should choose on purpose,
# not one it should apply silently.
#
# The consequence is real, and it is wider than this file's own package list:
# pkgsel/upgrade governs everything the installer selected, tasksel's standard
# set included, not just the @basePackagesCount@ names on the pkgsel/include
# line above. So a Stage-0 machine ends its install with all of that at the
# netinst image's versions, where a hand-installed Stage A machine may already
# be ahead by whatever point release landed since the image was built.
# RUNBOOK.md's Stage 0 tells the reader to run `sudo apt upgrade` afterwards if
# they want parity.
d-i pkgsel/upgrade select none
popularity-contest popularity-contest/participate boolean false

# The group memberships, generated from calango.bootstrap.groups, plus sudo.
#
# sudo is here and not only in the group list because Stage C runs sudo from the
# account's own shell. Without this line the runbook has to branch on whether
# the installer put the user in sudo, which it does only when root was given an
# empty password -- and that branch is where spec 18's rehearsal found two
# missing packages.
#
# in-target runs inside the installed system, after pkgsel, so nix-users exists
# by then: it is created by nix-setup-systemd's postinst, which pkgsel has
# already run. That was reasoned rather than traced until spec 19's rehearsal
# traced it, in the installer's own /var/log/installer/syslog, 19 seconds apart:
#
#   20:00:04 groupadd[30572]: new group: name=nix-users, GID=989
#   20:00:24 preseed: running preseed command preseed/late_command: in-target
#            usermod -aG nix-users,video,input,sudo isutton
#   20:00:24 usermod[5560]: add 'isutton' to group 'nix-users'
#
# Note usermod -aG is all-or-nothing: had the group been missing, the account
# would hold NONE of these, not all but one, so Stage C could not run sudo at
# all. Gate A is what catches that, and it stays in the runbook for that reason
# -- but read its group count with care. user-setup adds video to the first
# account by itself (one usermod per group, pids 5439..5487 in that same log),
# so only input, nix-users and sudo are this line's work.
d-i preseed/late_command string in-target usermod -aG @groupsWithSudo@ @username@
